\section{Checkpoints}{

With \code{control_horizons(output_dir = )}, each config's result is
written to \verb{<output_dir>/checkpoints/<config_id>.rds} as it finishes, and
a rerun into the same directory resumes from those files. Required when
configs are dispatched to workers. A resumed row must have been scored on
this run's training data (the outcome, the sample ids, which columns hold
the \code{id}, \code{outcome} and \code{predictor} roles, and the outcome and predictor
values) and tuned with this run's settings
(\code{cv_folds}, \code{grid_size}, \code{bayesian_iter}, \code{prune}, \code{prune_threshold}
when pruning, \code{seed}, and \code{configure()}'s \code{sg_window}, \code{pca_threshold}
and \code{outcome_range}) and this run's response trim
(\code{response_threshold}, \code{NA} when none was requested); either mismatch
aborts, naming what differs.
So re-standardized spectra, a rescaled outcome, or another outcome on the
same samples is refused rather than resumed, while adding a sibling
response with \code{add_response()} or a \code{meta} column resumes. Keep one
\code{output_dir} per outcome. A write to \code{output_dir} that fails (the
directory, the manifest or a checkpoint) stops the run with
\code{horizons_error_systemic}, naming the directory, the rows finished so
far as its \code{results}. On the configs axis a checkpoint a worker could
not write is known only once every configuration has run, so the
evaluation is returned, with one \code{horizons_warning_skipped} naming the
directory and the configurations a resumed run evaluates again. The
ranking \code{metric} is not checked, since
every row carries all six cross-validated metrics. A row scored under an
earlier scoring schema, or recording less of the training data and
settings than this run does, was written by an earlier version: it is
dropped and its config re-evaluated. Rows for configs no longer in the
grid are dropped; a file that cannot be used is a note in the object's
\code{log} and a line in the progress tree, and its config is re-evaluated.
}

\section{Parallelism}{

With \code{control_horizons(allow_par = TRUE)}, \code{evaluate()} dispatches onto
whatever \code{future::plan()} the caller has registered. If no usable backend
is registered (fewer than two workers), it runs sequentially and warns,
with class \code{horizons_warning_substitution}, naming the plan it found; the
warning is also a row of the object's \code{log}. Without it, \code{evaluate()} runs
in the calling process and never inspects the plan. It never registers or
alters a plan.

The user owns the backend and the topology; \code{evaluate()} owns only which
axis the plan is applied to, through \code{control_horizons(parallelize_over = )}.
\itemize{
\item \code{"configs"}: one future per config on the registered plan, with tune
running sequentially inside each. The per-worker payload is one config's
worth and tune ships nothing to inner workers. The mode for a large
config grid on a many-core machine.
\item \code{"resamples"}: configs run sequentially; inside each, tune parallelises
the CV folds on the registered plan. Useful width is capped at
\code{cv_folds}. The mode for a few cores and a short grid.
\item \code{"auto"} (default): \code{"configs"} when the number of configs is at least
\code{cv_folds} and there is an \code{output_dir} to checkpoint into, otherwise
\code{"resamples"}. Derived from the measured cost model: configs wins
whenever \code{n_configs} exceeds the effective inner speedup, which is
bounded by \code{cv_folds}, and always once it exceeds the worker count, so
the worker count cancels. \code{"configs"} asked for explicitly without an
\code{output_dir} is refused before anything runs (#233).
}

A nested plan (configs across an outer level, folds across an inner one)
is not built or endorsed by the package in this version. A caller who
wants one registers it with \code{future::plan(list(...))} and uses
\code{"configs"}.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{# Laptop: four cores, parallelise the folds inside each config.
future::plan(future::multisession, workers = 4)
hd |> evaluate(control = control_horizons(allow_par = TRUE, parallelize_over = "resamples"))

# Thirty cores, one config per worker, checkpointed.
future::plan(future::multisession, workers = 30)
hd |> evaluate(control = control_horizons(allow_par = TRUE, output_dir = "output/run"))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Before dispatch, BLAS, OpenMP, data.table and ranger threads are pinned to
one in the calling process (via \code{RhpcBLASctl} when installed) and restored
on exit. On either axis the workers load the \emph{installed} package, so a
parallel run refuses, before anything is drawn, under
\code{devtools::load_all()} or when a worker loads another version of horizons
than the session (class \code{horizons_error_dependency}). With
\code{control_horizons(debug = TRUE)}, \code{evaluate()} runs in this session
whatever \code{allow_par} says.
}

\section{Response outliers}{

When \code{validate()} was asked to remove response outliers
(\code{remove_outliers = TRUE} or \code{"response"}), it records the request and
removes nothing on labels. \code{evaluate()} draws its train/test split on the
untrimmed rows, computes Tukey fences (\code{validate()}'s
\code{response_threshold} times the IQR) from the training partition's
outcome values alone, and drops the training rows outside them before
the CV folds are drawn. Test rows are never removed on their labels, so
the test metrics describe the population the model will meet, extremes
included. \code{n_train} counts the training rows left after the trim, and
\code{evaluation$response_trim} records it: the \code{outcome}, \code{method} and
\code{threshold}, \code{fences_from} (\code{"training"}), the \code{lower} and \code{upper}
fences, \code{n_training} (the training rows the fences were computed over),
\code{trimmed_ids}, and \code{skipped} (\code{NA}, or why no fences could be drawn, in
which case nothing is trimmed and \code{evaluate()} warns). \code{fit()} reuses
the split and those ids. The CV assessment folds are drawn from the
trimmed training rows, so the cross-validated metrics (and the ranking
on them) describe the trimmed population; the test metrics do not.
}

\section{Failed configurations}{

Before anything runs, \code{evaluate()} refuses configurations whose model's
engine or feature-selection step needs a package that is not installed,
naming each package and what needs it (\code{horizons_error_missing_package}).
Each configuration runs as one unit. A failure in any of its steps fails
that configuration alone: its row has \code{status} \code{"failed"}, an
\code{error_message} naming the step and the cause ("Grid search failed:
..."), the root \code{class} of the failure, and, with
\code{control_horizons(keep_traces = TRUE)}, the backtrace as text in \code{trace}.
A recipe step that fails inside tuning is reproduced by prepping the
recipe once on the training part, so the step's own error is recorded with
its class; a failure tune keeps only as text is \code{horizons_error_run}. A
hyperparameter candidate scored on fewer cross-validation folds than were
drawn takes no part in the prune gate or the choice of hyperparameters,
and a configuration with no grid candidate scored on every fold fails; a
\verb{cv_*} mean is kept only when it covers every fold, so every ranked value
is on the same folds (#101). A configuration whose final fit on the
training part fails is failed too (#102). An error that is not the
configuration's own (an input or dependency error, or a bug in horizons)
stops the call. So does a failure every configuration would share: when
the first configurations to run fail the same way across at least two
models, \code{evaluate()} stops with \code{horizons_error_systemic}, the rows so far
as its \code{results} (\code{control_horizons(breaker = )}, 3 by default, \code{FALSE} to
run every configuration; the configs axis has no breaker), and their
failures are not checkpointed. A parallel worker that lacks a package the
configurations need, which this session has, stops the run the same way.
So does a parallel worker that dies, during a Bayesian iteration too: tune
catches future's error there, so the death is read from what future.apply
reports first
("Caught FutureInterruptError. Canceling all iterations ...", a warning
before future.apply 1.21.0 and a message since), which has no class and
is matched by its text. When some configurations failed and
the rest are returned, one \code{horizons_warning_partial_failure} names them
with each distinct cause. A configuration whose Bayesian search stopped
early keeps the iterations it finished; one whose search failed before any
iteration, or added nothing to the grid, keeps the grid's choice. Either
says so in its \code{warnings} and is named in one
\code{horizons_warning_substitution}. A configuration whose feature-selection
step kept every band (\code{horizons_warning_substitution}), confirmed nothing
(\code{horizons_warning_quality}) or had too few bands to select from
(\code{horizons_warning_skipped}) is named in that topic's warning, whatever
the model: what the step did is read from the final fit's recipe. With
\code{control_horizons(debug = TRUE)} nothing is contained: the first failure,
inside tuning included, stops the call with its backtrace, and the
Bayesian search is not fallen back from.
}

\section{When every configuration fails}{

\code{best_config} is chosen from the configs that succeeded or, when none did,
from the pruned configs that carry a cross-validated value of \code{metric}. A
config without a value of \code{metric} is left out of the ranking, with one
\code{horizons_warning_skipped} naming it. When configs were evaluated but none
has a value of \code{metric}, \code{evaluate()} returns with \code{best_config} \code{NA} and
that warning, and \code{fit()} asks for another \code{metric}.

When every config failed, \code{evaluate()} aborts with class
\code{horizons_error_all_configs_failed} (and \code{horizons_error_run}). The
message lists the distinct error messages (the first three, each with the
configs that raised it) and names any configs that were loaded from
checkpoints in \code{output_dir} rather than run, since calling \code{evaluate()}
again resumes them instead of re-running them. The per-config results,
\code{error_message} included, travel on the condition as \code{results}, because
\code{evaluate()} aborts before it assigns \code{x$evaluation}; after an uncaught
abort, \code{rlang::last_error()$results} recovers them without re-running.

A failure the first configurations share across at least two models stops
the call earlier, with \code{horizons_error_systemic} (also
\code{horizons_error_run}) and the rows finished so far as its \code{results}; with
\code{control_horizons(breaker = FALSE)} every configuration runs and the call
ends in the all-failed abort instead. Both carry \code{results}, so a loop over
subsets can catch \code{horizons_error_run}, tolerate a subset that fails
either way, and keep its errors:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{runs <- lapply(subsets, function(hd) \{
  tryCatch(
    evaluate(hd, control = control_horizons(verbose = FALSE)),
    horizons_error_run = function(e) e$results
  )
\})

# A horizons_eval for each subset that ranked, and the results table so
# far for each subset that failed.
failed <- runs[!vapply(runs, inherits, logical(1), "horizons_eval")]
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

